DXWeb / DXWeb Admin - Nginx reverse proxy examples
==================================================

These are generic examples for a native DXSpider installation. The HTTP example is derived from the configuration successfully validated on discovery (Nginx nginx -t, reverse proxy and WebSocket); the FQDN has been replaced by a placeholder. The HTTPS example has not been tested with real certificates.

Architecture

Public listener     Nginx upstream           Application
TCP 7380            http://127.0.0.1:7480    DXWeb
TCP 7381            http://127.0.0.1:7481    DXWeb Admin

Nginx listens on ports 7380/7381. The two application processes must listen on loopback only, on ports 7480/7481. Do not expose the application ports publicly. Configure the applications with MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=1; keep the DXSpider technical connection local. Ensure the application services are configured separately to use the ports above.

Choosing a configuration

- dxweb-http.conf.example: HTTP-only; matches the validated reverse-proxy behavior. Do not expose authenticated/admin use over public HTTP: credentials and sessions are not protected by TLS.
- dxweb-https.conf.example: HTTPS/WSS on the same public ports, with TLS terminated by Nginx. It replaces the HTTP configuration; do not enable both files simultaneously on the same ports.

Edit your.dxspider.example to the actual FQDN in both server blocks. For HTTPS, also replace /path/to/ssl/fullchain.pem and /path/to/ssl/privkey.pem with existing certificate/key paths. The certificate must cover the chosen FQDN. File permissions must allow the Nginx master process to read them. The example does not issue or renew certificates.

Installation on the test host (Nginx installed)

Copy one example into /etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb.conf and enable it using /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dxweb.conf. Back up any existing configuration first. For example, from the unpacked package:

sh
sudo cp /etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb.conf /etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb.conf.bak  # only if an existing file is present
sudo cp dxweb-https.conf.example /etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb.conf
sudo ln -sfn /etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dxweb.conf
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl reload nginx


Do not run nginx -t or reload with the HTTPS template until the certificate and key paths have been replaced with valid files. If you do not have certificates yet, use the HTTP example for local-only tests and leave the public Admin port closed. Avoid running both the distribution's default Nginx site and this example if they conflict.

For a TLS deployment, use https://FQDN:7380 and https://FQDN:7381. WebSocket upgrades become WSS externally, while upstream traffic stays HTTP on loopback. Configure DNS and firewall/NAT separately. No automatic HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect is provided because both protocols would otherwise compete for the same ports.

Validation

sh
sudo nginx -t
sudo ss -ltnp | grep -E ':(7380|7381|7480|7481)\b'
curl -I http://127.0.0.1:7380/       # HTTP example only
curl -I https://FQDN:7380/           # HTTPS example only, with valid DNS/certificate
curl -I https://FQDN:7381/           # HTTPS example only


Also verify WebSocket operation, authenticated access and the real client IP from an external client. curl -I is only a basic connectivity check, not a complete application test.

Repo documentation integration

Link to these two examples from dxweb/INSTALL and dxweb-admin/INSTALL under their Nginx reverse-proxy sections. Keep the HTTP/HTTPS distinction and the requirement to bind application backends to loopback. This package does not modify the existing repository files because their exact current contents have not been provided.

